      "sha256": "168056537e225368161f47a53596b460275c919cf737a3df0843fb8b7e72c3c9"
    },
    "authoritativeText": [
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n80",
        "locale": "zh",
        "text": "为了分清物体的细小部分，物体在眼前张开的角度，也就是视角，需要足够大。\n如果眼睛能无限地调焦，把同一物体移近，就能不断增大视角。\n但眼睛使像变清楚的调焦能力有限，物体太近时，视角虽然大了，像却可能变模糊。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n80",
        "locale": "zh",
        "text": "放大镜等光学仪器能增大视角，并能在光路和眼睛状态合适时，让眼睛放松调焦来观察。\n从图 185 的关系可以理解，在同一眼睛中，较大的视角对应视网膜上较大的像。\n\n当角度很小时，视网膜上像的尺寸之比近似等于视角之比。\n这说明仪器可以让细节在视网膜上分得更开，并不是把物体本身变大。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n80",
        "locale": "zh",
        "text": "通过仪器观察时的视角，除以肉眼观察同一物体时的视角，这个比叫作角放大率。\n它比较的是物体在眼前张开的角度，肉眼观察的距离也必须事先约定，才能作有意义的比较。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n80",
        "locale": "zh",
        "text": "在角度很小时，肉眼观察的视角（图 188）可以用物体高度与观察距离的比来近似表示，原书写成 $\\varphi=\\frac{h}{d_0}$ （6.20）。\n式中 $d_0=25$ 厘米是比较所用的明视距离，$h$ 是物体的高度或所比较的线尺寸。\n\n这个明视距离是初等光学的约定参考，不是每个人都相同的最近看清距离。\n这里的角度要用弧度这种角度单位表示，不能直接把以度数表示的角代入，长度也要用相同单位。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n80",
        "locale": "zh",
        "text": "图 188"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n80",
        "locale": "zh",
        "text": "图 189"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n80",
        "locale": "zh",
        "text": "观察细小物体时，放大镜是增大视角的一种简单工具。\n它可以是一枚会聚透镜，也可以是透镜组，焦距 $F$ 较短，原书描述常见值一般不超过 $10$ 厘米，这不是所有放大镜的硬性界限。\n这里先采用理想薄透镜的简化，把放大镜靠近眼睛，并把物体放在它的焦平面上。\n\n焦平面是经过焦点、与主光轴垂直的平面，物体处在这个位置时，同一物点发出的光经透镜后形成近似平行的光束（图 189）。\n不同物点的光束可以朝不同方向传播，因此物体的细节仍以不同方向进入眼睛。\n如果眼睛本来能在放松调焦时看清远物，它就能把这些近似平行的光束聚焦到视网膜上，不必额外调焦。\n\n在上述放置方式和小角度近似下，通过放大镜观察的视角写成 $\\varphi_1=\\frac{h}{F}$。\n这个式子描述光束在眼前张开的角度，不是说每个真实透镜都完全符合薄透镜的简化。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n80",
        "locale": "zh",
        "text": "按角放大率的定义，用放大镜的视角除以肉眼观察的视角，得到 $\\Gamma=\\frac{\\varphi_1}{\\varphi}$ （6.21）。\n比较时仍使用同一物体，以及前面约定的肉眼观察距离。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n80",
        "locale": "zh",
        "text": "原书的代入说明提到 $\\varphi_1$ 和 $\\varphi_2$，但这里的后一个符号没有得到清楚的定义。\n下面按原书保留计算式 $\\Gamma=\\frac{h/F}{h/d_0}=\\frac{d_0}{F}$ （6.22）。\n\n从这个算式可以看到，分子和分母中的物体高度约去后，结果是比较距离与焦距的比。\n这里还不能确定那个未定义的符号指哪个角，所以先读懂上面的计算，不把它当作另一种已经说明的角。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n80",
        "locale": "zh",
        "text": "把物体从焦平面略微向透镜移近时，放大率可以比原来的放置方式稍大。\n这时进入眼睛的光不再是原来那样的平行光束，眼睛需要调焦来把像看清。\n因此，这种变化是在放大程度与调焦需求之间作比较，不能把放松观察时的公式直接用于所有物体位置。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n80",
        "locale": "zh",
        "text": "原书指出，焦距很短的单片放大镜往往很小，用它观察不方便，因此放大能力受到实际使用条件的限制。\n但“只有直径小才有短焦距”不能当作普遍规律，焦距还与透镜表面的弯曲程度和材料有关。\n显微镜把观察分成两步，先让靠近物体的物镜形成一个放大的实像。\n\n实像处有光实际汇合，这个中间像可以成为后面观察的对象。\n再用靠近眼睛的目镜像放大镜一样观察这个中间像，就能进一步增大看到细节的视角。\n由物镜和目镜这样组合的仪器叫作显微镜。\n\n本课用简单透镜代表这些部分，真实显微镜的物镜和目镜可以包含多片透镜，光路设计也不只有这一种。\n这里要掌握的是先成中间实像、再由目镜观察它的顺序，而不是把简化结构当作所有显微镜的完整结构。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n80",
        "locale": "zh",
        "text": "在这个会聚薄透镜模型中，把物体放在物镜焦点与两倍焦距的位置之间，就能在另一侧得到放大的实像。\n再安置目镜，让这个中间实像落在目镜的焦平面上。\n这样，每个中间像点的光经目镜后成为近似平行的光束，能在放松调焦时看清远物的眼睛便不需额外调焦。\n\n图 190 用两条光线表示物点 $A$ 发出的光在中间实像点 $A_1$ 汇合。\n这些光再经目镜折射进入眼睛，最后由眼睛的光学系统形成视网膜上的像 $A_2$。\n因此要区分物体、中间实像和视网膜上的像，它们是光路中的不同位置。\n\n显微镜增大细节进入眼睛的视角，让原来肉眼难以分开的细小部分变得可以分辨。\n原书说这些部分在肉眼观察时“重叠”，这里指看不清它们之间的区别，不是说所有细节的像真的处在同一点。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n80",
        "locale": "zh",
        "text": "图 190"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n80",
        "locale": "en",
        "text": "To distinguish small details, the angle between the directions from the eye to their edges, called the visual angle, needs to be large enough.\nIf the eye could adjust its focus without limit, bringing the same object closer would keep increasing this angle.\nThe eye's focusing adjustment has a limit, however, so an object brought too close may look blurred even though its visual angle is larger."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n80",
        "locale": "en",
        "text": "Optical instruments such as magnifying glasses can increase the visual angle and allow relaxed focusing when the ray arrangement and the eye's focusing state are suitable.\nThe relationship in Figure 185 shows how a larger visual angle corresponds to a larger retinal image in the same eye.\n\nFor small angles, the ratio of retinal image sizes is approximately the ratio of visual angles.\nAn instrument can therefore spread details farther apart on the retina without making the object itself larger."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n80",
        "locale": "en",
        "text": "Angular magnification is the visual angle seen through an instrument divided by the angle seen when viewing the same object without it.\nIt compares the angle between the directions from the eye to the object's edges with and without the instrument, so the unaided viewing distance must also be specified."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n80",
        "locale": "en",
        "text": "For a small angle, the unaided visual angle (Figure 188) can be approximated by object height divided by viewing distance, written in the source as $\\varphi=\\frac{h}{d_0}$ (6.20).\nHere $d_0=25$ cm is the reference distance of distinct vision, and $h$ is the object's height or the linear size being compared.\n\nThis distance is a conventional reference in elementary optics, not the same nearest clear viewing distance for everyone.\nThe angle must be expressed in radians, an angle unit, rather than inserting a value in degrees directly, and the lengths must use matching units."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n80",
        "locale": "en",
        "text": "Figure 188"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n80",
        "locale": "en",
        "text": "Figure 189"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n80",
        "locale": "en",
        "text": "A magnifying glass is a simple way to increase the visual angle when examining a small object.\nIt may be a converging lens or a lens group with a short focal length $F$, commonly no more than $10$ cm in the source's description, rather than a strict limit for every magnifying glass.\n\nWe first use an ideal thin-lens model, placing the magnifying glass near the eye and the object in its focal plane.\nThe focal plane passes through the focus perpendicular to the optical axis, and an object there sends light from each point through the lens as an approximately parallel bundle (Figure 189).\n\nBundles from different object points can travel in different directions, so the details still enter the eye from different directions.\nAn eye that can already focus distant objects with its focusing adjustment relaxed can bring these approximately parallel bundles to focus on the retina without an extra adjustment.\n\nFor this arrangement and the small-angle approximation, the angle seen through the magnifying glass is written as $\\varphi_1=\\frac{h}{F}$.\nThis is an approximate formula for the angle at the eye and does not describe every real lens exactly."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n80",
        "locale": "en",
        "text": "By the definition of angular magnification, divide the magnifier's visual angle by the unaided visual angle to obtain $\\Gamma=\\frac{\\varphi_1}{\\varphi}$ (6.21).\nThe comparison still uses the same object and the previously specified unaided viewing distance."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n80",
        "locale": "en",
        "text": "The source's substitution instruction names $\\varphi_1$ and $\\varphi_2$, but the latter symbol is not clearly defined here.\nWe retain the source's calculation as $\\Gamma=\\frac{h/F}{h/d_0}=\\frac{d_0}{F}$ (6.22).\n\nIn this written calculation, the object height cancels between numerator and denominator, leaving the comparison distance divided by the focal length.\nBecause the second symbol is not defined here, we cannot yet tell which angle it means, but we can follow the calculation above without treating it as another already explained angle."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n80",
        "locale": "en",
        "text": "Moving the object slightly inward from the focal plane toward the lens can give a somewhat greater magnification than the original arrangement.\nThe light entering the eye is no longer the same parallel-bundle arrangement, so the eye needs to adjust its focus to see clearly.\nThis compares magnification with focusing demand, rather than applying the relaxed-viewing formula to every object position."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n80",
        "locale": "en",
        "text": "The source notes that a very short-focal-length single magnifier is often small and awkward to view through, placing practical limits on its usefulness.\nIts claim that only a small-diameter lens can have a short focal length is not a universal rule, because how strongly its surfaces curve and the lens material also affect focal length.\n\nA microscope works in two stages, first using the objective, near the object, to form an enlarged real image.\nLight actually meets at a real image, so this intermediate image can become the object for the next viewing stage.\n\nThe eyepiece, near the eye, then acts like a magnifying glass for that intermediate image and further increases the angle through which its details are seen.\nAn instrument combining an objective and eyepiece this way is called a microscope.\n\nThis lesson represents these parts with simple lenses, while actual objectives and eyepieces may contain several lenses and use other optical arrangements.\nThe important sequence here is an intermediate real image followed by eyepiece viewing, not a claim that the simplified structure describes every microscope completely."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n80",
        "locale": "en",
        "text": "In this converging thin-lens model, an object between the objective's focus and the position at twice its focal length forms an enlarged real image on the other side.\nPlace the eyepiece so that this intermediate image lies in the eyepiece's focal plane.\n\nLight from each intermediate image point then leaves the eyepiece as an approximately parallel bundle, so an eye able to see distant objects with relaxed focusing needs no extra adjustment.\nFigure 190 represents the light from object point $A$ with two rays meeting at intermediate real-image point $A_1$.\n\nThe light is then refracted by the eyepiece into the eye, whose own optical system forms the retinal image at $A_2$.\nThe object, intermediate real image and retinal image must therefore be distinguished as different positions along the light path.\n\nThe microscope increases the visual angle of details, allowing small parts that were difficult to distinguish without it to be separated.\nThe source calls those details overlapping during unaided viewing, meaning that their differences cannot be resolved, not that every detail's image is literally at the same point."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n80",
        "locale": "en",
        "text": "Figure 190"
      }
    ],
